[B]Memory & TPS:[/B] queue bounded (bounded by [I]cacheCap[/I]); each entry is a small POJO. Server TPS held at [B]20.00[/B] across every Paper and Folia run. On Spigot, every plugin saturates to the same chunk-gen ceiling; LeafRTP-Pro just spends those ticks doing fewer things.

- Paper LeafRTP-Pro row reproduced n=2; other rows are n=1 on a single rig. - JakesRTP Spigot row ran in slot 4 of a 4-phase chain - Min TPS / CPU-per-TP confounded by carry-over; dispatch-time numbers (throughput, p99) remain valid.

[B]Caveats.[/B] 2 clients only (the number is a floor, not a ceiling). Hardware, view distance, world state, and other plugins will move the numbers. Competitor plugins update frequently; corrections welcome via GitHub issue with a contradicting repro or doc link. Feature breadth, GUI, and claim-integration count are not benchmarked - several competitors trade speed for those, which is a legitimate choice.

Full methodology, raw CSVs, per-run analyses: [URL='https://github.com/dailystruggle/RTP/tree/V3/helpers/StressTestRTP'][I]helpers/StressTestRTP/[/I][/URL]. Video benchmark of [I]/rtp[/I] on a custom world generator: [URL='https://youtu.be/V0NyNK9JydM']youtu.be/V0NyNK9JydM[/URL].
[/SPOILER]

[SPOILER='Architecture - five pillars + chunk-loading by platform']
[B]Five pillars:[/B]
[LIST]
[*][B]Deterministic spiral selection.[/B] Bounded math, uniform player distribution even on massive worlds; no unbounded re-roll loops.
[*][B]Platform-native performance.[/B] Spigot, Paper, and Folia each get the threading model that fits them - not the lowest-common-denominator.
[*][B]Active resource watchdog.[/B] Background sweep reclaims chunk tickets from abandoned teleports via WeakReferences; nothing stays force-loaded past its reservation window.
[*][B]Direct region I/O pre-filter.[/B] [I].mca[/I] region files parsed off-tick on a background pool, including biomes (custom namespaced biomes like Iris included). Paper-grade safety evaluation on Spigot; skips per-candidate Region-Thread hops on Folia.
[*][B]Admin-driven background mapping.[/B] [I]/rtp scan start|pause|resume|reset|cancel[/I] walks the spiral during idle periods so spatial memory accumulates without player traffic. Fully-automatic self-warming is on the roadmap for [I]3.0.0[/I] final.
[/LIST]

[B]Chunk loading, by platform:[/B]
[LIST]
[*][B][COLOR=#27AE60]Paper[/COLOR][/B] - [I]World#getChunkAtAsync[/I] used directly. No pre-filter, no main-thread fallback. Reference platform.
[*][B][COLOR=#27AE60]Paper forks[/COLOR][/B] (Leaf, Leaves, Purpur, Pufferfish, Airplane, DivineMC, -) - inherit the Paper code path.
[*][B][COLOR=#2980B9]Folia[/COLOR][/B] - Anvil read-only pre-filter on [I]ForkJoinPool.commonPool()[/I] [I]before[/I] the Region Scheduler; rejected candidates never hop a thread. Confirmed candidates load through Folia's native async API; teleports dispatch through the Entity Scheduler.
[*][B][COLOR=#E67E22]Spigot[/COLOR][/B] - [I].mca[/I] region files parsed off-tick ([I]isAir[/I], [I]isSafe[/I], surface-height, sky-light, biome). Paper-class throughput on plain Spigot.
[*][B][COLOR=#27AE60]Mohist / Arclight[/COLOR][/B] - officially supported. Spigot code path applies.
[*][B][COLOR=#8E44AD]Fabric[/COLOR][/B] - first-class, stable, in-tree adapter; supported and regularly tested, at feature parity with the Bukkit family. Loom-remapped obf/unobf carriers cover 1.20.x, 1.21.x, and MC 26.x runtimes.
[/LIST]

[B]Honest fallback caveats.[/B] The Anvil pre-filter is a data source, not a universal gate. It falls through to the platform's native chunk API in two cases: (1) the chunk is already loaded (live data wins), or (2) the probe returns [I]unknown[/I] (no region file, unsupported data version, decode error, un-populated). On Spigot the fallback is one on-tick [I]getChunkAt[/I]. On Folia it's one Region-Scheduler hop. Custom generators (Iris, Terra, datapacks) do [B]not[/B] trigger the fallback - populated [I].mca[/I] palettes are read directly, preserving modded and namespaced IDs that the Bukkit enum would collapse.

[B]Upgrade-drift-proof biome filtering.[/B] When you bump your Paper or Folia server across MC versions, Mojang's seed-based biome assignment can change for already-written coordinates. The Anvil-first biome read keeps the [I].mca[/I] palette authoritative for populated chunks - the biome a player lands in is the biome your [I]biomes:[/I] allow-list was written against. No re-pregen, no re-tuning after an upgrade.

[B]Operator takeaway:[/B] Paper and Folia run chunk I/O fully off-tick. On vanilla Spigot the Anvil pre-filter covers the common case; rare main-thread fallback is bounded by your configured tick-budget; no platform silently blocks the tick loop without reporting it.
[/SPOILER]

[SPOILER='Spatial memory - why the longer it runs, the faster it gets']
Without spatial indexing, teleportation is a guessing game and the numbers are ugly. A typical Overworld is only [B]~45% safely teleportable[/B]- (oceans, rivers, ravines, steep terrain eat the rest); the Nether is dominated by lava seas and wall-to-wall stone; the End is [I]almost entirely[/I] void. Standard RTPs pay full chunk-load cost to rediscover this one candidate at a time, forever.

LeafRTP-Pro plots your world's geometry as it evaluates candidates. When it hits a massive ocean or unsafe biome, it remembers that sector and shrinks the searchable area, so known bad sectors never get loaded again. The longer it runs, the faster it gets - and [I]/rtp scan[/I] keeps the learning going during idle periods.

Watch the background scan crawl the spiral and accumulate spatial memory in real time: [URL='https://youtu.be/Ftjy1zw_S04']youtu.be/Ftjy1zw_S04[/URL].

[B]Persistence across restarts:[/B] learned bad-sector state survives a JVM bounce - [I]MemoryShape.save[/I] / [I]load[/I] writes per-region spatial memory to disk on shutdown and reloads on startup, alongside the configuration cache (compiled safety sets, region geometry). H2, SQLite, MySQL, and PostgreSQL backends support shared multi-server state.

[SIZE=2][I]- Local-profile estimate from a vanilla 1.21 seed set; Nether and End are still qualitative. Anonymous opt-in telemetry is on the roadmap to source this number properly.[/I][/SIZE]
[/SPOILER]

[SPOILER='safety.yml token grammar - Pro exclusive']
[I]safety.yml[/I] has a first-class token grammar. Six shapes can be mixed freely in [I]unsafeBlocks[/I] and [I]airBlocks[/I]:

[LIST]
[*][B]Plain material:[/B] [I]LAVA[/I], [I]MAGMA_BLOCK[/I].
[*][B]Material + state predicate:[/B] [I]OAK_SLAB[waterlogged=true][/I]. Multiple predicates AND together: [I]OAK_SLAB[waterlogged=true,type=top][/I].
[*][B]Numeric range predicate:[/B] [I]WATER[level>=5][/I], [I]LIGHT[level<8][/I]. Operators >= / <= / > / < bound fluid levels, light levels, and any integer block-state property; fail-open on an absent or non-numeric value.
[*][B]Vanilla block tag:[/B] [I]#minecraft:leaves[/I], [I]#minecraft:fire[/I], [I]#minecraft:campfires[/I]. Live-registry expansion at config-load lands in [I]3.0.0[/I] final - use explicit names alongside tag tokens during the beta.
[*][B]Tag + state predicate:[/B] [I]#minecraft:slabs[waterlogged=true][/I] - "any slab, but only when waterlogged".
[*][B]Wildcard + state predicate:[/B] [I]*[waterlogged=true][/I] - "any block, matched when waterlogged". One line replaces the entire waterloggable enumeration.
[/LIST]

[B]Properties:[/B]
[LIST]
[*][B]Fail-open on unknown tags/properties[/B] - missing tags on older MC versions silently reduce coverage rather than breaking startup. Configs stay portable.
[*][B]Never silent on malformed tokens[/B] - typos like [I]OAK_SLAB[waterlogged=true[/I] surface as [I][WARNING] [safety.yml] rejected token '<token>': <reason>[/I].
[*][B]Zero hot-path cost when unused[/B] - block-state extraction only fires when a token actually needs it; plain-material configs pay nothing.
[*][B]Platform-portable tag resolution[/B] - Bukkit reads from [I]Bukkit.getTag[/I]; the standalone [I]rtp-tags[/I] module parses tag JSON directly from data packs and jars for future non-Bukkit platforms.[/I]
[/LIST]


[I][B]Default safety.yml is ~25% shorter:[/B] [I]FIRE[/I]+[I]SOUL_FIRE[/I] -> [I]#minecraft:fire[/I]; every waterloggable block -> [I]*[waterlogged=true][/I]; dozens of decorative plants -> [I]#minecraft:flowers[/I], [I]#minecraft:saplings[/I], [I]#minecraft:crops[/I], etc.[/I]
[/SPOILER]

[SPOILER='Full feature suite & integrations']
[B]Engine:[/B]
[LIST]
[*][I]Any number of teleport regions per world; per-region shape (Square, Circle, Rectangle), radius, center, curve weighting, vertical bounds, world override, permission gates.[/I]
[*][I]Vertical adjustors (Linear, Jump, Fixed) for sky islands, void worlds, Nether ceilings.[/I]
[*][I]Multi-dimensional (Overworld, Nether, End, custom).[/I]
[*][I]Hot-reloadable YAML; clickable [I]/rtp menu[/I] (book on Paper / Folia, chat-paginated fallback elsewhere) hardened in 3.0.0-beta.3, with [I]/rtp config <file> view[/I] as a per-file deep-link.[/I]
[*][I]Fully async chunk loading on Paper / Folia; off-tick Anvil pre-filter on Spigot.[/I]
[*][I][B]Per-player isolated queues[/B] alongside a global queue - one player's bad luck never starves another's teleport.[/I]
[*][I]Administrative scan lifecycle ([I]start[/I]/[I]pause[/I]/[I]resume[/I]/[I]reset[/I]/[I]cancel[/I]) to pre-populate spatial memory without teleporting players.[/I]
[*][I]Persistent learned state via H2 / SQLite / MySQL / PostgreSQL.[/I]
[*][I]Per-tick time budgets and (on Folia) per-tick task-count caps on the region-bound pipe.[/I]
[/LIST]

[B]Player polish (UX):[/B]
[LIST]
[*][I]Configurable countdown/warmup messages during pre-teleport.[/I]
[*][I]Particles, sounds, fireworks, potions, note-block effects via [I]effects-api[/I], attached to lifecycle phases, gated by [I]rtp.effects.<name>[/I] permissions.[/I]
[*][I]Movement-cancel, damage-cancel, invulnerability-after-teleport timers.[/I]
[*][B]Optional PvP / combat-tag gate.[/B] [I]Off by default; when enabled, /rtp is refused or delayed for players who recently dealt or took PvP damage, so a teleport can't be used to escape a fight. Native combat tracking works out of the box, with optional PvPManager / CombatLogX / Simple Combat Log integration; the refusal message is fully localizable (messages.yml).[/I]
[*][I]Optional landing platform with configurable material and decay timer.[/I]
[*][B]Per-region arrival schematics.[/B] [I]Drop a [I].schem[/I] named after a region into [I]plugins/RTP/schematics/[/I] (e.g. [I]schematics/default.schem[/I]) and every teleport into that region pastes it - a lobby pad, arrival shrine, or custom platform - centered on the landing spot. Cross-platform Sponge [I].schem[/I], decoded in-house (no WorldEdit required), claim-aware (never overwrites protected land) and audited; the bundled Skyblock prefab ships one ready to go.[/I]
[*][I]PlaceholderAPI: queue depth (total/public/personal), last-teleport coordinates, player status.[/I]
[*][I]Public [I]rtp-api[/I] (same surface as Free) - trigger LeafRTP from GUI, NPC, quest reward; build your own UX without forking.[/I]
[*][B]Platform-agnostic addons[/B] [I]- addons compile against [I]rtp-api[/I] only and load through the [I]RTPAddon[/I] ServiceLoader SPI, so one addon module runs unchanged on Spigot, Paper, Folia, Fabric, and NeoForge. Config, safety hooks, teleport-pipeline callbacks, and scheduling are reached through platform-neutral APIs; you only add a platform module when the addon opens a platform-native UI. See the bundled RTP_ExampleAddon.[/I]
[/LIST]

[B]Bundled claim integrations[/B] (no extra download, folded in per ADR-019): Factions, GriefDefender, GriefPrevention, HuskTowns, Lands, RedProtect, TownyAdvanced, WorldGuard.

[B]Pro-only configuration:[/B] Vault per-region pricing, complete localization ([I]lang/**[/I]), login-reserve cache, visitor mode.
[/SPOILER]

[SPOILER='Roadmap - active development']
What's on deck, with direction:

[LIST]
[*][B]Spigot main-thread fallback diagnostics[/B] - per-minute fallback counters and an optional strict mode that skips the fallback entirely.
[*][B]Folia scheduler-hop telemetry[/B] - publish p50/p95 for un-pre-resolvable candidates and amortise across adjacent candidates where possible.
[*][B]Un-populated chunk attribution bucket[/B] - distinguish first-touch live loads from other fallbacks in the reports.
[*][B]Fully-automatic self-warming[/B] - background spatial-memory accumulation without the [I]/rtp scan[/I] verb.
[*][B]Anonymous opt-in telemetry[/B] - reference benchmark sourced from real deployments.
[/LIST]

File a GitHub issue if you hit something not on the list.
[/SPOILER]

[SPOILER='FAQ']
[B]Q: How do I stop /rtp from lagging my server, and why is LeafRTP-Pro faster than other random teleport plugins?[/B]
A: Pre-warmed queue. In most cases a verified destination is ready before you type [I]/rtp[/I]. Two design choices keep that queue cheap to refill: a [B]persistent spatial memory[/B] per region (the plugin remembers which sectors failed safety checks, so the spiral selector skips known-bad ground instead of rerolling indefinitely) and an [B]off-tick async pre-filter[/B] (Anvil region files are read directly to reject unsafe biomes/blocks [I]before[/I] any chunk is loaded, so candidate verification never blocks the main thread).

[B]Q: How do I set up teleportation between worlds?[/B]
A: See the admin guide. Resolution order: player's current world (or [I]world:[/I] param) -> world's target region -> region's target world.

[B]Q: Does LeafRTP work on Folia?[/B]
A: Yes, on both editions. The free build runs on Folia out of the box with a basic regionized scheduler (correctness-first: global/region/entity scheduling + async teleport). LeafRTP-Pro adds the tuned Folia adapter - the Region Scheduler with an off-tick pre-filter so no region stalls. Per-teleport work stays comfortably under one region tick; the 9.87 TP/s @ 99.97 % success we measured was limited by Region-Scheduler hops in the test harness, not by the engine's computation, so treat it as a harness-imposed floor rather than a performance ceiling.

[B]Q: Do you support triangle / diamond region shapes?[/B]
A: Use the [I]Polygon[/I] shape. A triangle is a 3-vertex polygon and a diamond is a rotated square, so both are already expressible without a dedicated shape type - define the vertices you want and the bounded spiral fills it.

[B]Q: Do I need Chunky (or another pre-generator)?[/B]
A: No. [I]/rtp scan start|pause|resume|reset|cancel[/I] is a built-in, off-tick generator that walks the region and builds persistent spatial memory. The philosophy is different from whole-world pre-generation: instead of loading every chunk up front, LeafRTP pre-verifies and remembers which sectors are unsafe, so it avoids loading bad ground at all. You can still run Chunky alongside it if you want a fully pre-generated map.

[B]Q: How does biome targeting behave on a pregenerated or upgraded world?[/B]
A: LeafRTP reads biome data from the populated [I].mca[/I] region files via the Anvil pre-filter, so [I]/rtp biome:<x>[/I] reflects the biomes actually written to disk. Plugins that lean on the live generator/noise-map biome lookup can return the [I]wrong[/I] biome on a world that was pregenerated elsewhere or migrated across a Minecraft version, where seed-based assignment has drifted - the Anvil-first read stays authoritative in those cases.

[B]Q: Iris / Terra / custom datapack generators?[/B]
A: Yes - deliberate 3.0 design goal. The Anvil pre-filter reads [I].mca[/I] palette data directly, so populated custom-generator chunks evaluate off-tick like vanilla. That's [I]strictly more accurate[/I] than the live Bukkit view, which collapses modded IDs to vanilla cousins. Un-populated chunks fall through to a live load as the authoritative safety net.

[B]Q: Existing safety.yml on upgrade?[/B]
A: Plain-material entries keep working unchanged; the new grammar is strictly additive.

[B]Q: Best LeafRTP plugin / setup for Paper 1.21 performance?[/B]
A: Paper, with memory to spare for the location cache. LeafRTP works particularly well on pre-generated worlds.

[B]Q: Can I downgrade to the free LeafRTP build?[/B]
A: Yes - same configuration, same data files, same commands. The free build still runs on Folia (basic regionized scheduling); what you give up is the [I]tuned[/I] Folia adapter, plus proxy, SQL/Redis, Vault, multilingual, and the tag/state-predicate grammar.
[/SPOILER]


[SPOILER='Support']
[LIST]
[*][B]Bug reports and configuration questions[/B] are welcome - the admin guide answers most setup questions, and a clear repro (server version, plugin version, platform, relevant configs and log lines) gets a fast resolution.
[*][B]Response time:[/B] 24-72 h on weekdays. Critical safety issues jump the queue.
[*][B]Feature requests[/B] via GitHub issues. Priority follows the published roadmap.
[*][B]Native NeoForge[/B] is supported on Minecraft 1.21.x / 26.1.x. [B]Native Forge[/B] is not supported - use Arclight or Mohist with the Spigot/Paper jar.
[/LIST]
